of Applied Physiology</italic></source><year>2003</year><volume>95</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>1297</fpage><lpage>1300</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12754175</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ho</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Pinto</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>SD</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Association between the CYP3A5 genotype and blood pressure</article-title><source><italic>Hypertension</italic></source><year>2005</year><volume>45</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>294</fpage><lpage>298</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15596575</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pickering</surname><given-names>TG</given-names></name><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name><name><surname>Appel</surname><given-names>LJ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Recommendations for blood pressure measurement in humans and experimental animals&#x02014;part 1: blood pressure measurement in humans&#x02014;a statement for professionals from the Subcommittee of Professional and Public Education of the American Heart Association Council on high blood pressure research</article-title><source><italic>Circulation</italic></source><year>2005</year><volume>111</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>697</fpage><lpage>716</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15699287</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Caro</surname><given-names>JJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Speckman</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Salas</surname><given-names>M</giv